WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.245
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.245 --> 00:00:03.120
ANNA FREBEL: It's a very
fundamental question

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.740
for humanity to ask,
where do we come from?

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:09.720
And we are, in some
sense, A) trying

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:15.261
to study that, and B) trying
to educate everyone about that.

00:00:15.261 --> 00:00:17.430
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on Chalk Radio,

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:20.010
we're talking star stuff.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.720
In this episode,
we're going to be

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:27.510
exploring stellar archaeology
and the origins of, well,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.530
everything.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:32.790
ANNA FREBEL: It all comes from
the cosmos, from the stars,

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:35.646
and from the
supernova explosions.

00:00:35.646 --> 00:00:38.070
SARAH HANSEN: Our guest
today is associate professor

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.350
of Physics, Anna Frebel.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:42.510
In a series of
short videos created

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:45.480
to go with her book Searching
for the Oldest Stars,

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:48.060
she reveals the
secrets of stardust

00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:50.715
and explains the cosmic
origins of the elements.

00:00:53.265 --> 00:00:54.180
ANNA FREBEL: Hello.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.680
My name is Anna Frebel.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.640
I'm a physics professor at MIT,
but I'm actually an astronomer.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.680
So I sit in the
Astrophysics division,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.640
and I search for the oldest
stars in the universe.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:05.890
And it's really cool.

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:08.280
And I think we're going to
talk a lot more about that.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.560
SARAH HANSEN: It is pretty cool.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.650
And what's even cooler is that
by the end of this podcast,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.870
you'll understand
what she does, even

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.120
if your prior experience
with astronomy,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.060
like mine, starts and ends with
gazing at the stars and saying,

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:24.390
wait, is that the Big Dipper?

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.570
And that's exactly why we
wanted to have her on this show.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.940
She's an expert
science communicator.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:34.200
She conveys complex topics
in inspiring and clear ways.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.480
As the title of
her book suggests,

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.280
much of Professor
Frebel's work is

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:41.430
rooted in learning more about
the origins of the elements

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:44.700
by studying the oldest
stars in the universe.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:48.340
I was curious how old
we were talking about.

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.850
ANNA FREBEL: We
want to figure out

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:54.300
exactly how element
formation proceeds.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.580
And you have to look at
different types of objects

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.010
in the cosmos to figure it
out because all the elements

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.210
are made there.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:03.360
Now, if you take the
sun, for example,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.090
the sun is about 4.6
billion years old.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.360
And the universe is about
13.8 billion years old.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.850
So the sun and its
content reflects

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:21.030
the state of the universe
4.5 or 4.6 billion years ago.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:25.230
Now, that's pretty cool that we
get this kind of information.

00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:27.030
But it actually is
much better for us

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.790
to look at stars
that are much older,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:33.270
that are formed sooner
after the Big Bang.

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.150
And that's why we look
at the oldest stars.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.220
By studying the
composition of these stars,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:42.090
we can then figure out what
produced all these elements

00:02:42.090 --> 00:02:44.460
that we're seeing in the stars.

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.800
SARAH HANSEN: This process
of looking to the oldest

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.400
stars for information about the
building blocks of the universe

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.800
is called "stellar archaeology."

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:56.726
And it helps us look into
the past, way into the past.

00:02:56.726 --> 00:03:01.470
ANNA FREBEL: We are
archaeologists of sorts.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.650
We are really good in
piecing together the past

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.560
because every distant object
whose light we're seeing

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:09.390
has traveled some time to us.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:13.950
And we are now seeing it as
when, for example, a galaxy was

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.240
in its baby stage,
we're seeing that now.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:18.840
So that lets us
explore the past.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:23.760
Now, these stars that we are
exploring are in the Milky Way.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.380
And that's really our
local cosmic neighborhood.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.390
The light travel time
within our galaxy

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:34.260
is thousands of years,
perhaps, at least to the stars

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:35.260
that we're looking at.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:37.440
So compared to the
age of the universe,

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.000
that's next to nothing.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:42.540
So we are not looking for
stars that are very distant

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:45.480
and whose light has traveled
to us for literally a gazillion

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.560
years.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.810
We are looking for
stars that are truly old

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:50.850
and still sitting there.

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.670
And again, by studying
their composition,

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.420
we can then learn what
the universe looked

00:03:57.420 --> 00:04:00.000
like at the time
of their formation

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.736
soon after the Big Bang.

00:04:02.736 --> 00:04:05.400
SARAH HANSEN: You might be
wondering how a scientist does

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:08.480
this sort of work, this
stellar archaeology, digging

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.770
into the farthest
reaches of the universe.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:13.650
You'd have to use some of the
best telescopes in the world.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.300
And it's definitely
not something

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.449
you could set up
in your backyard.

00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:21.029
ANNA FREBEL: You have to fly
to South America to Chile

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:24.060
because most of the big
telescopes are located there.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.800
Others are located in Hawaii.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.560
And while this sounds
like a vacation,

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.910
it's really anything but.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:31.320
[ANNA FREBEL LAUGHS]

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.590
So you usually fly
through Miami or Atlanta.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:36.540
And then you have
to get to Santiago,

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.340
to Chile, the capital of Chile.

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.310
And then you have to fly another
hour from there to La Serena.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.500
And then you get
picked up by a car,

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.130
and it drives you 2 and 1/2
hours up into the mountain.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:51.120
And you'll get there, and you
see the glistening telescope

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.610
domes on the horizon
waiting for you.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:57.180
And then it's like a tiny,
tiny little village up there.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.510
So there are guest rooms
for the observers where

00:05:00.510 --> 00:05:02.130
you'll sleep during the day.

00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.500
So thick curtains are a must.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:07.770
And when also you
get there are you'll

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.950
meet other observers from
the other telescopes.

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:12.540
You'll have your dinner.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.970
And then you go
to the telescope.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.280
And then you sit there
with lots of monitors.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.700
And you work together
with a telescope operator.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:24.400
They operate the actual
telescope and the dome

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:26.620
and make sure that you
don't break anything.

00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.000
[ANNA FREBEL CHUCKLES]

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.980
And then the astronomer
operates the instrument.

00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:35.520
So that's basically
a giant digital phone

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.000
camera or a spectrograph.

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.960
It's like a prism
where you split

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:42.060
the light into rainbow colors.

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.820
So you have your buttons to
press and decisions to make,

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:49.020
and, of course, the whole
strategy about what to observe

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.990
and how to spread things
out over the entire night

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:55.060
so that you use every single
second of telescope time

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:59.190
that you have because
it's expensive and never

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:00.300
enough telescope time.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.100
[ANNA FREBEL LAUGHS]

00:06:02.100 --> 00:06:05.310
And then you get tired,
but you keep going.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.110
And you get even
more tired, and you

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:10.720
keep going until the sun
literally comes up again.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:13.590
And at that point
in the sunrise,

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.670
you just go back to bed and
then sleep as much as you can

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:18.300
during the day.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.492
And then history repeats itself.

00:06:20.492 --> 00:06:22.950
But if the weather is bad and,
let's say, there are clouds,

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:27.270
or it rains, or it snows,
or something else, then

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:28.620
tough luck.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.756
You go home empty-handed.

00:06:31.756 --> 00:06:34.130
SARAH HANSEN: Now,
when Professor Frebel

00:06:34.130 --> 00:06:36.170
is at the telescope
down in Chile,

00:06:36.170 --> 00:06:38.335
she's getting a very
different perspective

00:06:38.335 --> 00:06:39.960
than when she's back
here in Cambridge,

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.250
Massachusetts because the
universe you can visualize

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:46.317
is different depending on
where you're observing from.

00:06:46.317 --> 00:06:47.900
ANNA FREBEL: We have
a different night

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:50.390
sky in the Northern and
the Southern hemisphere.

00:06:50.390 --> 00:06:53.570
And that's because of our
orientation in the galaxy.

00:06:53.570 --> 00:06:56.650
We live in a spiral galaxy.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:01.460
And so what we see as the "Milky
Way," in quotes, on the night

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:05.830
sky is actually the
next door spiral arm.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:07.850
And so that's why
we have a band.

00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:09.470
Now in the Northern
hemisphere, we're

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:14.150
looking at the spiral
arm that's behind us.

00:07:14.150 --> 00:07:16.930
So we're looking
outside of the galaxy.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.650
So we're looking out.

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.395
In the Southern hemisphere,
we're looking in.

00:07:21.395 --> 00:07:23.720
And so it's really
bright and shiny.

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:26.423
And that backlights
the spiral arm.

00:07:26.423 --> 00:07:28.340
And so when you're in
the Southern hemisphere,

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:33.230
you see a beautiful
bright band over the sky,

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:36.470
much unlike what we have
in the Northern hemisphere.

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:41.390
It's truly remarkable
how bright this can be.

00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:45.200
So there's time during
a single exposure

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.780
to sneak out and look at the
sky because I'm still a person.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:51.890
I'm not just a scientist.

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:55.520
And I do like
looking at the sky.

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:59.090
And so I have been just walking
outside with my flashlight

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:01.310
under the Milky Way.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:06.560
And you can actually switch off
your flashlight there and just

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:12.035
walk in the light of the Milky
Way, no moon, just starlight.

00:08:12.035 --> 00:08:13.910
You can totally find
your way around.

00:08:17.854 --> 00:08:20.720
SARAH HANSEN: I was curious to
learn more about how Professor

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:23.480
Frebel went from stargazing
with the rest of us

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:25.430
to stellar archaeology.

00:08:25.430 --> 00:08:29.720
What made her want to dig so
much deeper into our cosmos?

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:32.570
ANNA FREBEL: It's just
always been with me.

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:39.049
Ever since I was little, I knew
I wanted to work with stars.

00:08:39.049 --> 00:08:41.695
And I knew it wasn't planets,
and it wasn't galaxies,

00:08:41.695 --> 00:08:43.669
it was stars.

00:08:43.669 --> 00:08:47.540
And I can really only
describe what draws me to this

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:50.480
and always has is
the analogy to why

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:52.030
is blue your favorite color?

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.920
Why is it not red?

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.790
It's just more like a feeling.

00:08:55.790 --> 00:09:00.110
And it really hit me when
I learned about old stars.

00:09:00.110 --> 00:09:06.710
I did not know about old stars
specifically until I was 22.

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:10.550
But in Australia, I first
encountered old stars,

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.520
and I knew that that's it
for the rest of my life.

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:15.480
[ANNA FREBEL LAUGHS]

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:20.950
SARAH HANSEN: As
I shared earlier,

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.870
Professor Frebel is the
author of the book Searching

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:26.270
for the Oldest
Stars, which makes

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:30.110
this extremely complex
topic accessible to anyone

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:33.530
still retaining some of that
childlike wonder and curiosity

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:38.326
about the world around, or
rather above, all of us.

00:09:38.326 --> 00:09:41.010
ANNA FREBEL: These times
really speak for themselves.

00:09:41.010 --> 00:09:44.500
People need to have more
scientific literacy.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:49.140
And I think the forgotten point
is that so much wonder comes

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:50.340
along with it.

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:54.150
Information is power,
but it's also wonder.

00:09:54.150 --> 00:09:57.360
And I think it's really,
really important as a scientist

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.550
to make your work accessible.

00:09:59.550 --> 00:10:02.010
You don't need a
bachelor's in science

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:06.180
to understand that your body
is made of different things.

00:10:06.180 --> 00:10:10.720
And we always ask the question,
where does stuff come from?

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:12.610
Where does the money
in my wallet come from?

00:10:12.610 --> 00:10:14.800
That's what my
five-year-old is asking now.

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:17.190
So, yeah, from my bank account.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.970
But the story is,
obviously, a lot bigger.

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.500
And science is exactly
the same thing.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.170
I mean, I could talk to him
about the economy and the stock

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.330
market and make it really,
really complicated.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.920
But in the end is I
have a job, and it pays,

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:31.930
and that's the basic idea.

00:10:31.930 --> 00:10:35.310
And actually coming back to the
stellar archaeology concept,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:37.770
I think everyone
should really know

00:10:37.770 --> 00:10:39.900
what their ancestry is like.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:42.780
And we are doing the exact
same thing in the cosmos.

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.760
We're looking at
stellar generations

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.860
and what came before.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.650
And it's a very fundamental
question for humanity

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:51.660
to ask, where do we come from?

00:10:54.282 --> 00:10:56.580
SARAH HANSEN: Part of
Professor Frebel's approach

00:10:56.580 --> 00:10:58.340
involves pulling
back the curtain

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:02.080
on what astrophysicists actually
do on a day-to-day basis.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:04.695
And this is something she
feels pretty passionate about.

00:11:04.695 --> 00:11:06.720
ANNA FREBEL: The
Nobel Prize was won

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.940
for this and this discovery.

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:10.080
That's cool.

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:11.850
That's very impressive.

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:15.300
But is that really
the interesting bit?

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.270
I think for many people it's
actually much more interesting

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:23.070
to hear the journey about what
went all wrong on that path

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:24.880
to success.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:27.030
We like to just take
the success in the end

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:30.540
and throw away the
99 other percent,

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:33.540
but that's not what life
and reality is like.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:38.250
As I said, we go to the
telescope and it's cloudy.

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:42.070
But once I found something,
I made a big discovery,

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:46.040
then everyone literally
forgets how many struggles

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:47.820
you had getting there.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:53.480
It's great to have success,
but most of life is--

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.580
it's not success.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:58.210
It's hard work, and it's
facing the struggles.

00:11:58.210 --> 00:12:01.410
And I think reminding
people that even when

00:12:01.410 --> 00:12:04.560
you do have success
there's really much, much

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:07.026
more to the story.

00:12:07.026 --> 00:12:10.860
SARAH HANSEN: Professor Frebel's
videos on MIT OpenCourseWare

00:12:10.860 --> 00:12:13.410
are meant for anyone to
learn from regardless

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:16.466
of previous physics or
astronomy knowledge.

00:12:16.466 --> 00:12:18.730
ANNA FREBEL: It's
really for anyone.

00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:21.220
And the book is written
in the same way.

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:23.080
If you don't like a
chapter, you can just

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:24.800
go to the next chapter.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.310
You could even read
the chapters backwards.

00:12:27.310 --> 00:12:29.590
It's all set up
so it's completely

00:12:29.590 --> 00:12:31.600
interchangeable and flexible.

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:37.120
I think that children age
10 or 15 could probably

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:40.030
start watching this
because, again, it's

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:41.920
about the big questions.

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.710
If you don't understand a
sentence or the detail, man,

00:12:44.710 --> 00:12:46.240
don't worry about it.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:50.110
You want to wonder and
be amazed by something,

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.810
it's perfectly fine not
to understand the details

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:55.990
because it's not the details
that make you amazed.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:59.000
It's catching a
glimpse of something

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:03.190
bigger where you develop the
desire to understand more.

00:13:03.190 --> 00:13:06.760
And once you want to understand
more, why, details a drop

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:08.390
in the ocean.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:09.910
SARAH HANSEN: In
parting I wanted

00:13:09.910 --> 00:13:13.420
to go from looking to the
past to looking to the future.

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:14.950
What exciting things
are scientists

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:17.160
doing to pave the way
for more discovery

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:20.830
and to inspire joy and wonder
for generations to come?

00:13:20.830 --> 00:13:25.780
ANNA FREBEL: What I foresee
in the next 5 to 10 years

00:13:25.780 --> 00:13:28.750
is that this combination
of knowing more

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:33.910
about how the elements are all
created and on what timescales,

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:38.350
finding, of course, additional
old stars and at all ages,

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:41.320
using their motions on the
sky, and then combining

00:13:41.320 --> 00:13:46.750
that with large simulations for
the formation of the Milky Way.

00:13:46.750 --> 00:13:49.840
And so I think there's
going to be a lot of work

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:57.470
into really figuring out all the
puzzle pieces that put together

00:13:57.470 --> 00:13:59.640
make up the Milky Way.

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:02.080
And we've figured
out a few big ones.

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:04.470
But there are still
lots of little details

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:06.720
that we need to figure out.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.010
And I think that that's
going to be the big thing.

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:11.090
So the Milky Way
in the bigger view,

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.800
in a more holistic
view, that's going to be

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:15.530
the focus in the next 10 years.

00:14:19.657 --> 00:14:21.240
SARAH HANSEN:
Ultimately with the help

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:23.340
of educators like
Professor Frebel,

00:14:23.340 --> 00:14:25.140
astrophysics can
be a way to become

00:14:25.140 --> 00:14:27.390
more interested in
the world, an avenue

00:14:27.390 --> 00:14:29.850
for understanding our
place in a much, much

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:32.340
larger scheme of things.

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:34.770
If you are ready to learn
more about the cosmic origin

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.690
of the elements or
have students who

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.120
would be inspired by
stellar archaeology,

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.070
you can find Professor
Frebel's videos

00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:43.680
on our MIT
OpenCourseWare website.

00:14:43.680 --> 00:14:46.500
Our resources are always
free and remixable.

00:14:46.500 --> 00:14:49.780
So they're perfect for
use in your own teaching.

